{
  "summary": "This computer's protection is switched on, but it has fallen behind on updates: Windows has not installed a security update in 95 days, and the Defender virus definitions are 40 days old. Getting both up to date is the first thing to do, since together they leave the computer open to threats that are already known and fixed. There is also one extra account to check, which is likely normal but worth confirming.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Windows and Defender are not getting updates",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PATCH-STALE:03036687",
        "DEF-SIGNATURES-OLD:cd772dcf"
      ],
      "explanation": "Both findings point to the same problem: this computer is not keeping up with updates. The newest Windows update is from 2026-08-20, about 95 days ago. Microsoft releases security fixes every month, so several rounds of fixes for known weaknesses are probably missing. Defender's virus definitions are also 40 days old. Defender itself is in good shape: antivirus, real-time protection and tamper protection are all on. But it can only recognize malware it has definitions for, so anything newer than about 40 days could slip past it. Because both are stale at the same time, something may be stopping updates in general, so it makes sense to look at them together. The report shows the fix for each one; following them should bring both up to date."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize the account <computer>\\<account-2>",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account called <computer>\\<account-2> that has full control over the computer. This is often perfectly normal. Many people deliberately keep a separate account like this for making changes, which is good practice. It only matters if you don't recognize it, because an unexpected account with full control can be a sign that someone else got in. This scan wasn't compared with an earlier one, so it can't tell when the account was added. If you know what this account is, there is nothing to worry about. If you don't, see the report's guidance for this finding."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
